            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Overall Creative Performance Picture</h2>
<p>This week observed a common dilemma across multiple overseas teams: ad creatives maintain stable CTR between 1.5%-2.5%, outperforming industry benchmarks, but CVR (conversion rate) consistently stays below 2%, driving up cost per acquisition (CPA). One performance marketing manager for a utility app reported: "No problem with reach, money is being spent, but ROI just won't work out."</p>
<p>This "high impressions but low conversions" phenomenon essentially reflects a disconnect between creative appeal and product value delivery.</p>
<h2>The Fundamental Difference Between 'Impressions' and 'Conversions'</h2>
<p><strong>Creative that gets impressions</strong> have these characteristics: strong visual impact, attention-grabbing first 3 seconds, copy that sparks curiosity or emotional resonance. These creatives drive high click-through rates, but when users arrive and find content doesn't match expectations, bounce rates skyrocket.</p>
<p><strong>Creative that converts</strong> adds one critical element on top of the above: accurately conveying core product value. By the time users click, they already know what they'll get. These creatives may have lower CTR than the former, but conversion rates are often 2-3x higher.</p>
<p>This week's review found "high CTR, low conversion" creatives mainly concentrated in three scenarios:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Suspense-driven clickbait creatives</strong>: Creating curiosity without product information</li>
<li><strong>Pure discount-driven promotional creatives</strong>: Attracting deal-hunters rather than target users</li>
<li><strong>Celebrity/KOL appearances with vague product information</strong>: High traffic but low trust</li>
</ul>
<h2>Executable Diagnosis Methods for This Week</h2>
<h3>1. Build CTR vs CVR Comparison Tables</h3>
<p>Export data by creative dimension from ad manager, adding two calculated fields:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Effective CTR</strong> = Conversions / Impressions (not clicks)</li>
<li><strong>Value Match Score</strong> = CVR / CTR</li>
</ul>
<p>If a creative has CTR&gt;2% but Value Match Score&lt;0.5, it indicates the creative is "click-baiting" and you need to reassess the relationship between hook design and product value.</p>
<h3>2. Landing Page Heatmap Cross-Validation</h3>
<p>This week, prioritize checking landing pages corresponding to high CTR but low CVR creatives. Focus on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Whether above-the-fold content matches the core selling point of the ad creative</li>
<li>Whether CTA button is visible within above-the-fold area</li>
<li>Whether mobile load speed exceeds 3 seconds</li>
</ul>
<p>One independent website team discovered this week that their main creative displayed "limited-time discount" but the landing page above-the-fold showed brand story — users had to scroll down to see promotional information. This is a classic information gap.</p>
<h3>3. Audience Overlap Scanning</h3>
<p>Another often-overlooked issue discovered this week: multiple creatives for the same product running simultaneously with highly overlapping audiences, causing internal competition. Some clicks get "stolen" by your own other creatives, artificially inflating CTR while scattering conversions. Use ad set level tools to check audience overlap ratios — if exceeding 40%, consider consolidating or differentiating targeting.</p>
<h2>Action Recommendations for Next Week</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Creative Classification and Tagging</strong>: Add "hook type" tags in creative naming (suspense/problem-painpoint/benefit), making it easier to compare CVR performance by type later</li>
<li><strong>Landing Page A/B Testing Priority</strong>: Prioritize testing landing page variants paired with high CTR creatives, targeting a 20% improvement in effective conversion rate</li>
<li><strong>Budget Reallocation</strong>: Shift 20%-30% budget from "high impressions, low conversion" creatives to historically proven creatives with both CTR&gt;1.5% and CVR&gt;3%</li>
</ol>
<p>The core conclusion this week: creative review can't rely on single metrics alone. Getting impressions is the foundation; getting conversions is the goal. When your account shows signals of "normal reach but losing ROI," the first thing to check isn't bidding or targeting — it's the match quality between creative value delivery and landing page experience.</p>
